\subsection{Measured Artifact Metrics}
\label{sec:artifact-metrics}
To complement the qualitative execution-stage observations, the artifact
extension was instrumented to count the concrete artifacts produced under
each configuration. All values were measured after a single
\texttt{ArtifactManager.inject\_all()} call using the
\texttt{demo\_artifacts/} directory. The baseline and
network-only configurations produce no host-side artifacts by design;
therefore the corresponding cells are zero.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Measured artifact metrics across configurations}
\label{tab:artifact-metrics}
\begin{tabular}{|L{0.32\textwidth}|L{0.16\textwidth}|L{0.18\textwidth}|L{0.22\textwidth}|}
\hline
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Baseline} & \textbf{Network only} & \textbf{Network + artifacts} \\
\hline
DNS cache entries (\texttt{cache.txt})    & 0 & 0 & 157 \\
\hline
Distinct TTL values                       & 0 & 0 & 151 \\
\hline
Browser history URLs                      & 0 & 0 & 25 \\
\hline
Distinct visit transitions                & 0 & 0 & 6 \\
\hline
Zone.Identifier files                     & 0 & 0 & 5 \\
\hline
Linux artifact files                      & 0 & 0 & 18 \\
\hline
Linux \texttt{machine-id} (hex chars)     & 0 & 0 & 32 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\noindent\textbf{Sampling method.} DNS entry count was derived from the
\texttt{Record Name} marker in \texttt{dns/cache.txt}; distinct TTL values
were extracted from the \texttt{Time To Live} lines using a set difference.
Browser URL and transition counts were read from the SQLite
\texttt{urls} and \texttt{visits} tables of \texttt{browser/History}.
The Linux file count is the recursive file total under
\texttt{linux/}, and the \texttt{machine-id} value is the length in
hexadecimal characters of the generated identifier.

