\subsection{Proof-of-Concept Test Verification}
\label{sec:poc-verification}
The artifact extension was independently verified through a dedicated
proof-of-concept (PoC) test suite shipped with the module. The suite
covers the three configurations described in Section~\ref{sec:eval-setup}
through 12 test cases: 3 baseline scenarios (Case~A clean sandbox, Case~B
service-only simulation, Case~C full injection) and 9 detailed checks
covering DNS cache, browser history, transition diversity,
\texttt{Zone.Identifier} generation, Chrome epoch correctness, and the
newly added Linux artifact creation. The suite was executed with
\texttt{pytest -v}; all 12 cases passed.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{PoC test suite summary (12/12 passed)}
\label{tab:poc-tests}
\begin{tabular}{|L{0.04\textwidth}|L{0.56\textwidth}|L{0.18\textwidth}|L{0.14\textwidth}|}
\hline
\textbf{ID} & \textbf{Test case} & \textbf{Scenario} & \textbf{Result} \\
\hline
A1 & \texttt{test\_case\_a\_clean\_sandbox\_detected}                & Baseline   & PASS \\
\hline
A2 & \texttt{test\_case\_a\_individual\_checks}                       & Baseline   & PASS \\
\hline
B1 & \texttt{test\_case\_b\_service\_only\_still\_detected}            & Network    & PASS \\
\hline
C1 & \texttt{test\_case\_c\_artifact\_injection\_bypasses\_detection}   & Enhanced   & PASS \\
\hline
C2 & \texttt{test\_case\_c\_dns\_checks\_pass}                         & Enhanced   & PASS \\
\hline
C3 & \texttt{test\_case\_c\_browser\_checks\_pass}                      & Enhanced   & PASS \\
\hline
D1 & \texttt{test\_artifact\_freshness\_dns}                            & Enhanced   & PASS \\
\hline
D2 & \texttt{test\_zone\_identifier\_created\_for\_download}            & Enhanced   & PASS \\
\hline
D3 & \texttt{test\_zone\_identifier\_fields\_populated}                 & Enhanced   & PASS \\
\hline
D4 & \texttt{test\_chrome\_timestamps\_in\_correct\_epoch}              & Enhanced   & PASS \\
\hline
L1 & \texttt{test\_linux\_artifacts\_created}                          & Enhanced   & PASS \\
\hline
L2 & \texttt{test\_linux\_artifacts\_are\_fresh\_across\_runs}          & Enhanced   & PASS \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\noindent The test IDs A1--C3 correspond to the three baseline scenarios
defined in the original prompt. The D-series tests verify anti-fingerprinting
properties (freshness, Zone.Identifier structure, Chrome epoch) and the
L-series tests cover the Linux artifact extension. The complete output
of \texttt{pytest -v} is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:screenshot-pytest}.

% =====================================================================
% SECTION 3 - New subsection: Reproducibility
% Insert immediately AFTER "PoC Test Verification"
% =====================================================================

\subsection{Reproducibility Across Runs}
\label{sec:reproducibility}
Two dedicated tests verify that consecutive injection runs produce
statistically distinct outputs, preventing the ``bulk-injected'' fingerprint
and demonstrating that the system does not produce identical artifacts on
every execution. \texttt{test\_artifact\_freshness\_dns} asserts that
two independent injection runs yield different DNS cache contents (driven
by randomised TTL values and event ordering), while
\texttt{test\_linux\_artifacts\_are\_fresh\_across\_runs} asserts that
the \texttt{machine-id} differs between runs (generated from
\texttt{secrets.token\_hex(16)}).

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Freshness comparison across two consecutive injection runs}
\label{tab:freshness}
\begin{tabular}{|L{0.32\textwidth}|L{0.28\textwidth}|L{0.28\textwidth}|}
\hline
\textbf{Property} & \textbf{Run 1} & \textbf{Run 2} \\
\hline
DNS cache byte length      & 34{,}800 bytes & 34{,}799 bytes \\
\hline
TTL standard deviation     & 1{,}071.37 s   & 1{,}076.25 s   \\
\hline
Browser URL count          & 25             & 25             \\
\hline
Linux \texttt{machine-id}  & \texttt{563442d699fd38374db12258f4eaac94} & \texttt{c4f2c2d71b05dae591b8fee2be717e97} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\noindent This property is essential for evading fingerprinting routines
that look for repeated or static values across multiple invocations of the
same sample. The DNS cache contents and Linux \texttt{machine-id} differ
between the two runs, confirming that the artifact extension remains
unpredictable at the granularity of an individual run. The browser
URL count is identical (25 entries) because the URL set is currently
drawn from a fixed list of plausible domains; the \emph{freshness} of
the HTTP history is provided instead by randomised
\texttt{last\_visit\_time} timestamps (Chrome epoch) and the randomised
\texttt{visit\_duration} fields, which together prevent the
``synthetic transition'' fingerprint checked by
\texttt{check\_transition\_uniform}.

