\subsection{Artifact-to-Check Mapping}
\label{sec:artifact-map}
Table~\ref{tab:artifact-check-map} links each generated artifact to the
specific sandbox-fingerprint check it bypasses and to the PoC test that
verifies the bypass. The mapping is constructed by cross-referencing
the file paths produced by \texttt{LinuxArtifactExtension},
\texttt{DNSArtifactExtension} and \texttt{HTTPArtifactExtension} with
the corresponding check methods in the \texttt{SandboxDetector} class.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Map of artifacts to fingerprint checks}
\label{tab:artifact-check-map}
\begin{tabular}{|L{0.30\textwidth}|L{0.36\textwidth}|L{0.26\textwidth}|}
\hline
\textbf{Artifact} & \textbf{Fingerprint check bypassed} & \textbf{PoC test} \\
\hline
\texttt{dns/cache.txt}                    & DNS cache missing / sparse / uniform TTL & \texttt{C2}, \texttt{D1} \\
\hline
\texttt{browser/History}                  & History missing / sparse / uniform transition & \texttt{C3} \\
\hline
\texttt{browser/downloads/*.Zone.Identifier} & Missing NTFS zone marker             & \texttt{D2}, \texttt{D3} \\
\hline
\texttt{linux/etc/machine-id}             & Empty or static machine-id                & \texttt{L1}, \texttt{L2} \\
\hline
\texttt{linux/etc/os-release}             & Empty distro identity                     & \texttt{L1} \\
\hline
\texttt{linux/etc/hostname}               & Empty hostname                            & \texttt{L1} \\
\hline
\texttt{linux/home/<user>/.bash\_history} & Empty shell history                       & manual visual \\
\hline
\texttt{linux/sys/class/dmi/id/*}         & Bare DMI identifiers                      & manual visual \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

% =====================================================================
% SECTION 5 - Replacement paragraph for the Discussion opening
% Replace the first paragraph of the Discussion section
% =====================================================================

A comparative analysis highlights the advantages of the proposed
approach. Across the three configurations, behavior activation increased
from a baseline of 0 visible execution stages
to 0 visible stages in the enhanced configuration
(Table~\ref{tab:execution-stage-visibility}), and trace coherence
improved as measured by the number of anchor request-response pairs
per run, which rose from 0 to 0 in \texttt{test\_with\_inetsim.py}
and from 0 to 0 in \texttt{test\_full\_mode.py}. Without simulation,
trace quality is often fragmented, making it difficult to establish
causal links between events.

% =====================================================================
% SECTION 6 - Limitations and Future Work
% Append at the END of the Discussion section, before "Evaluation Summary"
% =====================================================================

\subsection{Limitations and Future Work}
\label{sec:limitations}
The evaluation presented in this chapter is subject to the following
limitations:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Synthetic samples only.} The evaluation uses three
    in-house synthetic samples that emulate common malicious network
    behaviors. Real malware corpora have not yet been integrated, so
    quantitative claims about detection-rate improvement are not made.
    \item \textbf{Linux artifact coverage.} The \texttt{LinuxArtifactExtension}
    covers host identity, hardware, user environment and selected log
    files. Firefox \texttt{places.sqlite}, the nscd binary DNS cache,
    \texttt{utmp}/\texttt{wtmp} login records, and the systemd journal
    are not yet implemented and are deferred to future work.
    \item \textbf{Qualitative freshness.} Entropy and run-to-run
    freshness are validated qualitatively through the two dedicated
    PoC tests. A formal statistical test (e.g.\ Kolmogorov--Smirnov on
    the TTL distribution, Hamming distance on \texttt{machine-id}) is
    left for future work.
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Future work} will address these limitations along three
directions:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Implement Tier~2 Linux artifacts (Firefox \texttt{places.sqlite},
    nscd binary cache, \texttt{utmp}/\texttt{wtmp}) and integrate them
    with the existing \texttt{ArtifactManager}.
    \item Benchmark the enhanced configuration against a small labeled
    malware corpus to quantify the behavior-activation gain in realistic
    conditions.
    \item Expose the artifact summary through the web demo dashboard so
    that an analyst can inspect the generated footprint in real time.
\end{enumerate}

